<!--
  move.xml: navigate to a pose on the map's octomap.bt (OctomapPlanner3D,
  planner_id="Octo3D") with FollowPath (MPPI), replanning only when the
  current path becomes invalid, with contextual recovery.

  The only navigation tree since 2026-10-28. From 2026-10-19 this was
  move_3d.xml, run in place of a 2D move.xml (GridBased on the gridmap, dev's
  tree) while the planner's Octo3D had a map; 2D navigation was removed and
  this tree took the plain name, so a goal with no behavior_tree, or one
  naming move.xml, runs it. git history has the 2D tree and its header.

  Based on nav2's navigate_w_replanning_only_if_path_becomes_invalid.xml but
  without its outer system-level RecoveryNode: a planner / controller failure
  clears its own obstacle memory and retries once, since stale marks are the
  most common cause. nav2's system-level branch (clear both costmaps,
  Spin/Wait/BackUp via RoundRobin, abort on GoalUpdated) is left out for now;
  most of its BT nodes and the behavior server are not ported yet. The case
  BackUp is for, a robot boxed in so that every MPPI candidate collides, is
  the controller's own since 2026-10-25: FollowPath backs out up to
  back_out_dist once per path (controller_server_params.yaml), so each
  FollowPath retry below starts from where that left the robot.

  Planner. Octo3D routes over the floor surfaces of octomap.bt and between
  floors over a stair link. The controller, FollowPath (MPPI), matches a path
  with heights in 3D and climbs stairs with syncai_util::StairMode, the
  optimizer skipped on the flight and running everywhere else, so MPPI's
  local detours run on the local costmap's terrain layer, which judges steps,
  slopes, drops, stairs and low obstacles against the ground under the robot.
  A flight itself is climbed blind: no obstacle avoidance on stairs, by
  decision (2026-10-20). FollowPathRPP3D (RPP with the same 3D matching and
  stair mode, the first controller to do either) is still loaded, and
  controller_id="FollowPathRPP3D" below is the whole A/B.

  Why not replan every tick (the 2D tree did, at 1 Hz, until 2026-10): a
  fresh plan always wins, so the route is only as stable as the obstacle
  memory, and half-way round a detour the original route read free and
  shortest again and the robot turned back into the blocker. A path is kept
  until IsPathValid says the part still ahead is blocked, or the goal
  changes. The price: a blocker that leaves does not shorten the route back;
  the detour is finished. That is the recoverable side, and no "switch when
  the new path is X% shorter" threshold had to be invented to get it.

  IsPathValid is judged by Octo3D (2026-10-21): planner_server's
  is_path_valid asks the planner that made the last plan, and Octo3D answers
  on its dynamic overlay (syncai_global_planner, dynamic_overlay.hpp) -- the
  lidar cloud in the map frame, every return 0.1-0.6 m above the surface it
  lies on a mark on that cell (the surface is picked by height, so two
  floors never see each other's people), rays clearing the marks they cross,
  marks forgotten after 30 s. A replan detours: the search keeps off the
  marks and 0.35 m round them and pays for the band beyond as it does for a
  wall, so the route bends round a person with the room it has. Two rules of
  the check are the planner's: it judges only the floor stretch the robot is
  on and answers "valid" while the robot is on a stair, because a replan
  from mid-flight would snap its start to the wrong floor and a goal
  aborting on a flight stops the robot there; and the 0.35 m round the
  robot's own cell is exempt from the marks' dilation (as the planner
  exempts the start), so a person beside the robot does not block the pose
  under it with nothing a replan could change. A person inside the 0.25 m
  wall ring is not marked at all; the terrain layer and MPPI deal with them.
  From 2026-10-19 to 2026-10-21 the 3D tree planned once per goal
  (number_plans == 0 in place of IsPathValid): the check then ran on the 2D
  global costmap, where a path upstairs lay over the ground floor's gridmap
  and read blocked every second.

  The 1 Hz RateController paces the checks; a plan only runs when one fails.
  IsPathValid gets 100 ms instead of the 20 ms default_server_timeout. A
  timeout keeps the path (a replan nobody can justify is the thing this
  tree exists to avoid; the next check is a second away and the controller
  holds the robot meanwhile). A timeout WARN in the task_runner log on every
  tick means the check is slower than that: raise server_timeout here.

  A blocked path is replanned only once it has stayed blocked for 10 s
  (blocked_time, 2026-10-14): the same spot of the same path (within 0.5 m,
  blocked_match_distance) answered blocked by every check for that long,
  with no clear answer in between. Until then the path is kept, the
  task_runner log says "blocked at (x, y) for n of 10 s; keeping it" and the
  status sentence says the robot reroutes if it is still blocked after 10 s;
  one clear answer ends the wait without a replan. Before that day one answer
  was a replan, so a person walking across bought a detour that stuck for the
  rest of the goal: on robot01 (2026-10-14) a ~11 m leg flipped to a ~35 m
  detour and back, one blocked answer per switch. Three checks in a row
  (~2 s) was tried first that day; 10 s also waits out a person who stops in
  the way, which is when a detour is least wanted. Meanwhile MPPI bends round
  the blocker or stands in front of it. "0" is the old behaviour. The wait
  restarts on any new path, so it also spaces out a replan whose own result
  reads blocked. blocked_time is bounded by FollowPath's retries below:
  lengthen one, re-check the other.

  FollowPath gets four retries, and they refill (retry_refill_time, 2026-10).
  The count is how long the robot waits for a blocker that stands in front of
  it. MPPI bends round a blocker beside the path inside the local costmap;
  one that closes the way leaves it no feasible trajectory ("Optimizer fail
  to compute path"), and there is no Wait recovery, so from then on the wait
  is failure_tolerance (3 s) times retries + 1, with a local costmap clear
  between attempts (re-marked within a scan; the controllers zero their
  accel baseline on every refusal, so no lurch). One retry (~6 s) was enough
  for a person walking across and not for one standing and talking; three
  (~12 s) covered that, at the price of a task failure taking ~12 s instead
  of ~6 s to declare. Four (~16 s, 2026-10-14) because the wait has to
  outlast IsPathValid's blocked_time (10 s): a person who steps in close
  makes MPPI refuse at once, while the 1 Hz check can take a little longer
  to report the path blocked, so the replan lands ~13 s into the refusals
  (estimated, not measured). Keep (retries + 1) x 3 s at least blocked_time
  + 4 s (15 s would need six). In practice MPPI stands still legally in
  front of a person who stands still and never trips failure_tolerance, so
  the progress checker's 30 s movement_time_allowance is the outer bound.
  The refill: the RecoveryNode only resets when it returns, and FollowPath is
  RUNNING for the whole goal, so the retries used to be per goal -- a blocker
  met early used them up and the next one, however much later, failed the
  goal outright. An attempt that ran at least 60 s before failing now earns
  them back. 60 s is twice movement_time_allowance: an attempt that never
  gets going fails sooner than that, so a dead end still runs out of
  retries; it must not drop to 30 or below. The planner branch's
  RecoveryNode keeps one retry and needs no refill: ComputePathToPose
  returns each tick, which resets that node.

  Obstacle memory. Both ClearEntireCostmap nodes in the planner's half call
  planner_server/Octo3D/clear_overlay (nav2_msgs/srv/ClearEntireCostmap, the
  costmap clear's own type, so the node is the stock one): every new goal
  starts from a clean memory (2026-10-14, then on the 2D global costmap: on
  robot01 the return leg of an A <-> B shuttle came out 60 m instead of
  17.5 m, round marks the previous leg had left behind) -- a mark the robot
  walked away from is not raytraced away, and though it ages out after 30 s
  a goal sent sooner must not plan round a person who has left -- and a plan
  that fails clears and retries once. Only at tree start, so a preempt (the
  tree keeps running) does not clear: the memory that makes a detour stick
  is the point mid-goal. ForceSuccess, because a clear that times out must
  not fail the goal -- the stale-memory route is still a route. Octo3D does
  not wait for anything after a clear, so the first plan after it can run
  through a person in view until the next scan (<= 0.1 s) marks them and
  IsPathValid replans after blocked_time. Accepted: the stale-memory route
  is the worse failure. Side effect: ClearEntireCostmap counts itself into
  number_recoveries, so the feedback's number_of_recoveries starts at 1 on
  every goal (nothing reads it today). The FollowPath recovery clears the
  controller's local_costmap; relative service names resolve under the
  robot namespace because every node in the stack shares it.

  Failure attribution (2026-10). The navigator fills NavigateToGoal's
  error_code from the blackboard keys failed_node / failure_msg, which
  ComputePathToPose and FollowPath write when their goal is aborted (last
  writer wins; blackboard_keys.hpp). The tree's shape is what makes that
  correct, so it is load-bearing: PipelineSequence ticks the planner branch
  (child 0) before FollowPath (child 1) and returns at the first FAILURE. If
  the planner branch fails the tree, child 1 is not ticked in that tick, so
  ComputePathToPose's second abort is the last write. If FollowPath fails the
  tree in a tick where ComputePathToPose also aborted (its retry pending
  inside a RecoveryNode that returns RUNNING), child 1 is ticked after child
  0, so FollowPath wrote last. Either way the key names the branch that
  actually failed the tree, and a node that aborted and was retried
  successfully is overwritten by whichever node fails later. The
  ClearEntireCostmap nodes stay silent on purpose: a recovery one only runs
  because an action node just failed, so the last action-node write already
  names the right branch, and a service timeout there would otherwise
  overwrite it with something that maps to UNKNOWN. (The new-goal clear runs
  before either branch and, under ForceSuccess, never fails the tree, so it
  has nothing to name either.) Accepted limitation: a successfully retried
  abort leaves its entry behind, so a tree that later fails through a path
  that writes nothing (an exception) reports the stale one.

  Status sentence (2026-10). NavigateToGoal's feedback status_msg is the
  blackboard key nav_status_msg, written at each turn of this tree so the
  console can say more than "running": IsPathValid ("Path blocked;
  replanning") and GlobalUpdatedGoal ("Goal changed; replanning") when the
  planner branch is about to replan, ComputePathToPose when a path lands
  ("Following the path") or the planner gives up, FollowPath when the
  controller gives up and when its retry goes out. Same last-writer-wins
  channel as above, and the same child order makes it read right: a replan
  started by child 0 is written before FollowPath is ticked, and FollowPath
  only overwrites its own retry sentence, so "replanning" stands while the
  robot keeps driving the old path, until the new one lands.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Sequence name="NavigateFromFreshGlobalCostmap">
      <ForceSuccess>
        <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearDynamicOverlay-NewGoal" service_name="planner_server/Octo3D/clear_overlay" server_timeout="1000"/>
      </ForceSuccess>
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
        <RateController hz="1.0">
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
            <Fallback name="FallbackComputePathToPose">
              <ReactiveSequence name="CheckIfNewPathNeeded">
                <Inverter>
                  <GlobalUpdatedGoal/>
                </Inverter>
                <!-- Judged by Octo3D on its dynamic overlay (the planner server
                     asks the planner that made the last plan); see the header. -->
                <IsPathValid path="{path}" server_timeout="100" blocked_time="10.0"/>
              </ReactiveSequence>
              <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="Octo3D"/>
            </Fallback>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearDynamicOverlay-Context" service_name="planner_server/Octo3D/clear_overlay"/>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="4" retry_refill_time="60.0" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>
    </Sequence>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
